A||||7||NASAReeves||Hot engine comment||||4||
Z||000000||NASAReeves||05-20-2001||08:27 AM||||I've been reading a lot lately about the excessive engine temperature of the V-6.  I can attest to that, as it is difficult to check the oil level at the gas station, everything is so hot in there.<P>Is this a design flaw, or is it 'supposed' to run like this?  I'm considering installing something to cool her off, too.  Sounds like another order for Rodney...<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||4
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-20-2001||09:15 AM||||if you want your engine compartment to run cooler all you have to do is remove one connector and four bolts<P>the ones holding the engine lid on (c:<P>I took my lid off friday to work on the motor, and have driven it around a little between 'adjustments'<P>its also a great way to see how much your engine is flexing, you can adject your rear view mirror to see the intake plenium.<P> I think engine compartment heat is a perception thing, because the muffler and heat exchanger are both under there, and when you stop all that heat rises up out of the black vents on the sides of the lid.<P>  Maybe we sould do some testing an see if a fiero engine compartment really is hotter than other cars. I could test my saturns and see how hot it gets in there - all we need to do it tiewrap a meat thermometer in there and check it under various conditions.<P>  would be an interesting forum data collection effort.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Topknot||05-20-2001||09:19 AM||||In referrence to the heat issue,  one question I've always had was the vents located in the rear with the deck lid...Now do they vent heat out..being that heat rises..or do they help air to "flow" thru the engine compartment?<P>Now I have been tossing around the idea of creating some custom venting with some styles of fans and tubing to help larger amounts of air to flow thru...<P>Topknot =]<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||05-20-2001||11:42 AM||||The vents on the decklid are exit vents.  Air should come OUT of them, not go in.  It uses the airflow over the car to create a suction and the hot air's natural convection to drive the flow.  I'm thinking of adding a fan to mine, though.  Saw the info on Ogre's cave.||||reg||6
Z||000004||sjp777||05-20-2001||12:26 PM||||Drill out the rivets on the underside of the vents and take off the dripguards. Without them you vent at least twice as much hot air and it is a very noticable difference in engine compartment cooling<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||05-20-2001||09:12 PM|||| Other than leaves and bird pooh what are the downsides to removing the drip plate? Also on my '84 the vent directs hot air toward the back glass and it gets very hot. Any way to turn them around and still get them to work adaquetly?<BR> Don & Jane<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||FieroJoe||05-21-2001||10:47 AM||||Actually, I dont mind that my rear window gets hot, because the engine will defog the window if it ever steams up.<P>This is on my 87.  Most of the heat comes out the gap that is between the decklid and the window.  There used to be a weatherstrip in there to seal that gap, but it was removed in the recall.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000007||CC Rider||05-21-2001||11:04 AM||||read the heat managment article in fiero tunnel for a more detailed coverage.... The Ogre's Fiero Cave<BR> <A HREF="http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/index.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
